Below is a minimal, self‑contained Java‑style snippet that shows how to invoke the **`SearchableSnapshotDirectory_create`** helper with the objects you already have in scope.  
The helper expects *string representations* of the Java objects, so we simply pass the variables (the tool will call `toString()` on them for you).

```java
// 1.  Prepare the arguments – all of these are already initialized in your code.
String repositoryName          = "daily-snapshots";
String shardPath                = "/data/nodes/0/indices/logs/5";
String indexSettingsForLogs    = /* your IndexSettings instance */;
Supplier<Long> currentTimeNanos = /* your supplier */;

// 2.  Call the helper.  The tool will convert each argument to its string form.
Object searchableSnapshotDir = functions.SearchableSnapshotDirectory_create(
    new java.util.HashMap<String, Object>() {{
        put("repositories", repositoriesService);          // RepositoryService
        put("cache", cacheService);                       // CacheService
        put("indexSettings", indexSettingsForLogs);       // IndexSettings
        put("shardPath", shardPath);                      // String
        put("currentTimeNanosSupplier", currentTimeNanos); // Supplier<Long>
        put("threadPool", threadPool);                    // ThreadPool
        put("blobStoreCacheService", blobStoreCacheService); // BlobStoreCacheService
        put("sharedBlobCacheService", sharedBlobCacheService); // SharedBlobCacheService
    }}
);

// 3.  Use the returned directory (type depends on your implementation)
System.out.println("Created searchable snapshot directory: " + searchableSnapshotDir);
```

### What the snippet does

| Step | What happens | Why it matters |
|------|--------------|----------------|
| **1** | We keep the variables you already have (`repositoriesService`, `cacheService`, etc.) and the shard path you specified. | Keeps the code tidy and re‑uses existing objects. |
| **2** | We build a `Map<String,Object>` with the exact keys the helper expects (`repositories`, `cache`, `indexSettings`, …). | The helper signature requires a single map argument; the keys must match exactly. |
| **3** | The helper returns an instance of the searchable‑snapshot directory (the concrete type depends on your environment). | You can now use this object to interact with the snapshot (e.g., open files, read metadata, etc.). |

> **Tip** – If you’re working in a language other than Java (e.g., Kotlin, Scala, or a scripting language that interoperates with the JVM), the same idea applies: build a map with the same keys and pass it to the helper. The tool will handle the string conversion automatically.

Feel free to adapt the snippet to your exact class names or to wrap it in a method if you need to create directories for many shards in a loop.
